  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1245 PM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nWV imagery indicates a closed upper low transitioning east \nthrough the high plains of western Kansas and the panhandles of\nOklahoma and Texas. Near the surface, a stalled frontal \nboundary attendant to a low in extreme southwest Kansas extends \nnortheast into southeast Nebraska. \n\nBased on radar trends/CAMs, ongoing showers/thunderstorms will\ncontinue to develop southward across much of southwest Kansas \nthis afternoon, then end west to east later this evening as the \nSREF indicates a closed upper low opening up as it begins to \naccelerate eastward through the Central Plains tonight, giving \nway to a more zonal flow aloft across the Western High Plains \nFriday. The main focus the next several hours will be for \npossible flash flooding as already saturated ground from \nprolonged rainfall the previous day lends to a heightened \nflooding potential through early evening. This is supported with\nmost models showing high QPF signals as well as SPC \nMesoanalysis indicating PW values nearing 1.5 inches. A much \ndrier pattern is forecast to take hold across the region through\nthe weekend as a developing upper level ridge begins to amplify\nacross the Intermountain West while much drier air in the \nlower/mid-levels dominates the Western High Plains. \n\nThe coolest temperatures of the season are likely tonight as \ncooler air begins to spill into western Kansas behind an \nadvancing cold front, dropping H85 temperatures below 10C across\nmuch of southwest Kansas by daybreak Friday. According to the \nlatest HREF, there is a better than 90% probability of \ntemperatures slipping below 50F in west central Kansas into \ncentral Kansas in vicinity of the I-70 corridor to a 50-70% \nprobability of temperatures dropping below 55F in south central \nKansas. So, look for lows down into the 40s(F) northwest to the \nlower 50s(F) southeast. For a change, unseasonably cool \ntemperatures are forecast Friday as departing surface high \npressure through the Upper Midwest reinforces a cooler air mass \nin place across the high plains of western Kansas within a \nnortheastly upslope flow, holding H85 temperatures down around \n10-12C. With the HREF painting a widespread 90% probability of \ntemperatures exceeding 60F, expect afternoon highs up into the \n60s(F) with the warmest highs in south central Kansas where \nthere is a 80-90% probability of temperatures topping 65F. \nRebounding temperatures are to be expected Saturday as surface \nhigh pressure continues to push farther off to the east through \nthe Great Lakes while lee side troughing redevelops in eastern \nColorado, returning southerlies to western Kansas. This will tap\ninto slightly warmer air to our south, helping push H85 \ntemperatures above 13-15C. Under ample sunshine, expect more \nseasonable temperatures with fairly widespread 70s(F) for highs.",
